  <!-- NUTRITION -->
  <div class="panel" id="nutrition">
    <div class="note-box iron">
      <b>Iron.</b> You're taking an iron supplement, and this plan layers food-level iron on top of that: liver, beef, black/red beans and chickpeas carry most of it. Plant and organ iron absorbs better next to vitamin C, so pomegranate, orange, kiwi or a squeeze of vinegar-dressed cabbage rides along with those meals on purpose. Coffee, tea and calcium-rich foods (kefir, yoghurt, cottage cheese) block iron absorption if eaten in the same sitting — keep those as separate snacks rather than side dishes to the iron-heavy meals, and it's worth taking the supplement itself away from coffee/tea and dairy for the same reason. Keep checking iron levels periodically with your doctor since food plus supplement together can add up.
    </div>
    <div class="note-box vitd">
      <b>Vitamin D.</b> You're supplementing vitamin D as well. Salmon, tilapia and egg yolks are the only real food sources in this pantry, so they're spread across the weeks as backup rather than clustered in one place. Ten to fifteen minutes of midday sun most days helps too.
    </div>
    <div class="note-box chol">
      <b>Cholesterol.</b> Liver is nutrient-dense but concentrated in dietary cholesterol, so it appears once this month, blended into a beef base rather than eaten alone. Ground beef is capped at two batches for the month — choose the leanest grind available. More than half the batches lean on fish, tofu, chickpeas or beans instead of red meat. Oats, chia, ground flax and beans bring soluble fibre, and the nuts, avocado and olive-based cooking give you unsaturated fat in place of saturated fat where it's easy to swap.
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <b>Rough protein target.</b> For building muscle at 3–4 training days a week, most people do well aiming somewhere around 0.7–1g of protein per pound of bodyweight per day, spread across meals. This plan's lunches and dinners are built to land in the 35–45g protein range each; breakfast and snacks add the rest. Adjust portion sizes up or down against your own weight and appetite.
    </div>
    <div class="note-box">
      <b>Batch sizing.</b> Each recipe below is written to yield about 8 servings — enough for dinner the night it's cooked plus four more lunch/dinner servings across the following two days for two people. Scale up slightly if your workouts are heavy or portions run large.
    </div>
    <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--flex);margin-top:20px;">General nutrition information, not medical advice — for anything related to your iron, vitamin D or cholesterol levels specifically, check in with your doctor or a dietitian.</p>
  </div>

  <footer>4-Week Mediterranean Muscle Plan — cook twice, eat well all week.</footer>
